recently i streamed a no death run of  sakura over on twitch  defeating every boss on the way to  immortal severance without a single  resurrection used  now obviously there were a few failures  before this  some were understandable like this death  to the guardian aid i'm dead  i'm actually dead and  some are in places that i didn't even  know it was possible to die  [Music]  but then on run six i did the impossible  i died to the divine dragon they said it  couldn't be done  you only have to run in one direction  they said but you know against all the  odds  i did it and then on the seventh run we  switched the language to the obvious  choice for better  immersion french and turns out  this was le run i do you can watch the  vod over on twitch if you like  and to highlight the feeling of facing  secure's last boss  myself and a really renowned artist  called nogi  came up with this ink panel styled  design that's  available for a limited time on t-shirts  and hoodies link in the description  but i'll talk about it later so why  choose secure for a run like this  well it's because i think sakura has  the best combat of any souls game hands  down  and even though almost every one of my  runs ended in despair  i still looked forward to actually  playing the game and starting again  also i feel like this challenge was a  good way to celebrate the secular one  game of the year and we haven't talked  about that  and i also knew that this run would be  the best way to more deeply  understand what optimal combat looks  like perfect example on screen  actually don't look at that never mind  okay i had to cheese a few bosses to get  this run  but for the most part secure played  perfectly looks good  really good fully understanding a boss  and exploiting all of their weaknesses  it's an art in this game and it's so  satisfying to get a flawless fight  i think that in sakura the combat of  souls sees a way forward  sekido taught us that removing stamina  in a souls-like game  is completely fine as long as you  replace it with other complex systems  and  what better system did they add than the  timed block mechanic  perfect blocks deplete enemy posture  which eventually sets them up for a  death blow  and i'll admit that if eldon ring  doesn't have  some sort of timed block system like  this then  i'll be a little bit disappointed  honestly there's a lot of value  in designing a system that lets us stand  our ground against our enemies  and not just roll around at their feet  in full plate armor  since elton ring is supposed to be more  like dark souls maybe  timing your block might have different  consequences like stopping enemy attack  combos it could  give you a free hit it could reduce  their stamina  it could maintain your stamina there's a  lot they could do  and there's a lot future titles could  learn from sakura  and i think that brings us to the topic  of difficulty  shortly after sakura's release in 2019  there was this  really unproductive discussion about the  difficulty of the game  on twitter and in articles and both  extremes of this debate  really dominated the conversation people  antagonized each other and  an interesting discussion was really  difficult to have  and i never said my thoughts at the time  but here they are  while i personally love how  uncompromisingly difficult  sakura is i can see how it isn't for  everyone  just like sakura has parries and reposts  this  topic has a lot of interesting arguments  and counter arguments so  let's go through some of them first an  important premise to understand  is that everyone experiences difficulty  differently  whether it's your play style your  experience your gear your reflexes  what i find hard might not be hard for  you and vice versa  thus the sense of accomplishment we feel  is relative  and so many people argue that having a  variable difficulty mode  will allow players to make these games  as hard as they like  so that they can better align their  sense of accomplishment  with their own skill level but is this  really a choice that we want  players to be making if demon souls back  in 2009  allowed players to just turn on easy  mode whenever they were having trouble  with a boss  so many players would have taken that  easy way out and they would have denied  themselves a sense of accomplishment  as a result of that and that might have  led to the game  never becoming renowned for its  difficulty and that might have led to  the soul series never becoming  successful in the first place  so you can argue that this goes against  the design goals of the developers  and you can argue that it goes against  the very roots  of the game itself also choosing a  difficulty setting is not always a good  experience for the player  how often have you played a game and  then found an easy mode that's so easy  that you can just beat it by pressing  one button  or maybe you've found a hardcore mode  that's so unfair that you actually have  to  cheese all of your encounters in both of  these cases you aren't properly using  the gameplay mechanics  the way that the developers intended and  having one true difficulty mode makes it  much easier for the developers to  balance the ideal experience and to push  you towards playing a certain way  that they have deemed to be the right  way to play this encourages you to  properly use  all of the gameplay tools that the devs  have put at your disposal  and get the full experience out of the  game but the counter argument here is  that souls games have always had  variable difficulty a lot of people  don't bring this up but  if you're having trouble against a boss  you can just summon help  if you're too weak you can easily look  up overpowered weapons  or spend time grinding your character's  levels to make them stronger  so how is this any different to adding  an easy mode some people might say  well i think what makes this different  from adding an easy mode  is that you're being encouraged to find  your own answers within the game  when you summon someone to help you with  a boss fight you're forging a connection  and learning skills from other players  when you seek out a  overpowered weapon for example you're  learning that the world  has answers to difficult bosses if you'd  only go out and explore more  these things absolutely make the game  easier and they absolutely trivialize a  lot of the fights  but at least you are taking an active  role in changing the difficulty and you  are playing the game in a way that  allows you to change the difficulty  and often it just helps you understand  how to play  however this brings me to sakura since  the game is offline you can't look for  help  or learn from players around you and if  you spend time  chasing powerful upgrades or tools to  get you through a fight  it's kind of just time wasted that  should have been spent  practicing the fundamentals of combat  instead because  you're gonna need those fundamental  skills later in the game  so i do think in regards to difficulty  secure could have done more  on this front it is a shame that a lot  of people probably just wanted  a little bit of extra help when they  were stuck somewhere and  it's a shame that those people miss out  on the other things that make these  games so great like  the stories and the world building and  the immersion  these things would be open to so many  more people if sakura just had a little  bit more give  in it but as it stands the game locks  out a lot of players who  just don't have the time to dedicate to  mastering its mechanics  notice how i said time not skill there  because that brings me to my final point  which is  skill is a factor of time and it's a  factor of how dedicated you are to  overcoming adversity and your own  shortcomings  yes we all experience difficulty  differently and  some have much more to overcome than  others and they should be commended for  doing that  but i believe that almost anyone can  master these games  if they set their mind to it this game  will give back to you if you give it  your time  there's so many examples of that and  surely that's a well-known fact at this  point  so don't be like the elitists who  gatekeep our community and don't be like  the writers who pretend  that practice isn't possible rise above  encourage people and  help others understand why these games  are worth setting aside time  for so does sekiro deserve some  criticism  of course it does but to me this game  deserves game of the year because  it's stuck uncompromisingly to from  software's vision and  that is a trait that i hope they never  lose besides if we ever disagree with  their vision  then mods will save us  anyway i know this video is a bit  different from my other ones  i just sort of wanted to talk about  sakura again since it won game of the  year  and i wanted to make you all aware that  i stream on twitch sometimes now  and i'm planning on doing some more runs  like this in the future  so go and follow over there and unlike  all the other souls games  i'm happy with everything i covered on  sakura and  despite covering secure really  thoroughly though i realized i never  commissioned merge that was really  inspired by it  and who better for this task than nogi  an incredible artist who does these  beautiful hand-drawn  ink prints we made something that was  inspired by the final clash  in secure and it's presented in this  stylish  panel almost comic book style and it's a  design that we're really proud of  and it's available on hoodies on a shirt  and for this week only  you can get the red kanji edition which  is available just this week  and then never again but before i go i'd  also like to give shout outs to  distortion keg witty squealer killer and  charlie bit me these are streamers who  taught me a lot about how best  to complete my run their streams are  mainly challenge runs and speed runs and  watching them is so exciting all the  time because you never know when they're  gonna break the new record  so go and check out all the links in the  description and follow some of these  folks  and maybe consider how you yourself  might want to  chase this sense of accomplishment by  doing a run of your own  but thanks for watching and i'll see you  next time
 

